WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.197
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.197 --> 00:00:07.780
JAMIE SIDEY: Today
we're going to talk

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.040
about how to save 80% or
more by migrating your data

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.350
center into GCP.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.180
My name is Jamie Sidey.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.220
I'm chief of staff
of Optiva, and I've

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.600
been here for about two years.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.240
And I've been focused
on the operational side

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.670
of private equity
for about five years.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.480
My role is to look at
the cost and expense

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.460
side of the businesses
that we get involved in.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.790
Typically, these are financially
distressed, bankrupt,

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:34.470
out of cash.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.470
No other private equity
or venture capital group

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.960
wants to touch them.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.860
So we were very excited
when we found Optiva.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.410
It was deeply
financially distressed,

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.240
but the core product
was really good,

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.892
which made it a great fit
for our type of turnaround.

00:00:48.892 --> 00:00:50.350
KEITH MILLAR: And
I'm Keith Millar.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.040
I'm the senior vice president
of Pythian Services Business,

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.680
and we were the
services provider

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:01.215
who was chosen to help Optiva
in this migration to GCP.

00:01:01.215 --> 00:01:02.840
JAMIE SIDEY: So just
to set the stage--

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.239
what is it that Optiva does?

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:08.110
When you place a phone call or
use data on your mobile phone,

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.940
there's a split second
where your carrier

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.790
has to determine who are you?

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:13.930
Are you prepaid, postpaid?

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:15.888
Are you one of my
subscribers, somebody else's?

00:01:15.888 --> 00:01:17.410
How am I going to get paid?

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.870
It's not a particularly
sexy piece of software.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.070
But it's super important.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.590
If it goes down, then
either the carrier

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.460
has to not let
anything go through.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.820
And then all the subscribers
get super grumpy.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.990
Or the carrier lets
things go through,

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.020
but they don't get paid.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.470
So uptime is super important.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.600
In terms of what we're
bringing to the table post

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.230
turnaround for innovation,
we're taking this and moving it

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:42.940
to public cloud.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.900
So telco is a very
conservative risk averse space,

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.280
and they also have to
provision for peak load.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.958
So not anything particularly
new, but from our perspective,

00:01:55.958 --> 00:01:57.500
well, why don't you
move to somewhere

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.047
where you can provision for the
load that you actually need?

00:02:02.047 --> 00:02:04.130
So instead of provisioning
for Mother's Day, which

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:05.930
happens once a year
and as multiples

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.860
of what they need
the rest of the year,

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.610
they can scale up
and scale down.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:14.220
So we have 100 plus
customers all over the world,

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.400
billions of transactions
per hour at peak.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.962
Here's some of our customers.

00:02:17.962 --> 00:02:20.420
One of the things that's been
interesting for me in getting

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.420
involved in this business,
I follow world events a lot

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:24.830
more closely than I used to.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:29.482
So if there's a coup, or
a civil war, or whatever--

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.190
I mean, we have
customers in Afghanistan,

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.590
and Yemen, and Venezuela,
all over the place.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:36.110
And I'm much more aware
of what's happening now

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:39.787
because it impacts our
customers immediately.

00:02:39.787 --> 00:02:41.370
KEITH MILLAR: Sure,
I'll just give you

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.300
some insight as to Pythian's
sort of the opportunity

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.410
we see on Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:53.760
We're 20 years in business,
400 employees, 350 customers

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:54.280
globally.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.480
But it really kind of wraps up
in the love your data tag line.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.110
So we've always been good at
focusing on where the data is

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:01.990
or where it's going.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:05.040
And right now, we see a
lot of data going to GCP.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.680
So that's what we're excited
about with the Google

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.240
opportunity.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:11.530
What do we do?

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.560
What does Pythian do on GCP?

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.090
There's really two
main scenarios.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:19.260
One is the one we're talking
about today with Optiva,

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:23.760
moving bare metal or VMs
from on-prem into GCP.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.560
And that can be either on to
Google Cloud Compute Engine

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.240
or onto Kubernetes.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.490
So that's mostly what we're
going to talk about today.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.530
The other piece is
there's a lot of optimism

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.410
in the market around
what the cloud provides

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.200
as a next generation
analytics platform.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.310
So leveraging things
like BigQuery on GCP,

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:49.930
linking your legacy data
sources to that data warehouse,

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:52.500
and then using tools
like Look or Tableau

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.030
to gain those
insights once you've

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:57.900
got the real time streaming
infrastructure in place.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.080
For all of those scenarios,
there's really some key things

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.430
that we talk about all the time.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.730
Security, how do you set
up Google Security Console

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:10.080
from day one and have people
attending to those alerts 24/7?

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.730
Same with op vis, Stackdriver,
setting that up on day one,

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.010
and then dealing
with those 24/7.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.100
And really, the order of
magnitude change or complexity

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.310
that comes with Cloud is
quite apparent to us, so

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:24.630
evolutionary services.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.570
Once you've got a stack
set up in GCP coming in

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.218
on a quarterly basis and saying,
OK, here's what's changed.

00:04:30.218 --> 00:04:32.010
Here are the new pieces
that are available.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:33.690
Here's the ones we
suggest you drop,

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.920
or here's the new
ones that you adopt.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.530
So that's just
sort of how Pythian

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.448
looks at the GCP opportunity.

00:04:41.448 --> 00:04:43.740
JAMIE SIDEY: So what was the
mess that we started with?

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.780
When we got
involved, the company

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:50.310
spending about 15 million a
year on data center, which

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.430
with a base of revenue of about
150 million and the company

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:58.020
wasn't a SaaS company, 15
million a year is a lot.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.700
10 data centers,
tens of thousands

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.740
of square foot of space,
millions a year on electricity,

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:08.040
100 engineers just to
supporting the data centers.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.780
This type of location
proliferation

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.340
is not actually
unusual when you've

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.040
got a company that's formed
by multiple successive

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:14.760
acquisitions.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.220
Each division that comes in
brings with it its own data

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.760
centers, its own
team, and each team

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.340
feels that they have specific
unique expertise that's

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.900
very hard to replace.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.228
Which they do have
specific expertise,

00:05:27.228 --> 00:05:28.770
and it is hard to
replace, but if you

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.690
don't have the discipline to
consolidate, then over time,

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.230
eventually you get to
spending 15 million in a year

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.970
and just hemorrhaging money.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:39.420
So our big idea,
for those of you

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.850
who read Built to
Last back in the 90s,

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.890
the BHAG, the big,
hairy, audacious goal

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:46.830
was to save 90%, going
from 15 million a year

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:50.112
to 1 and 1/2 million
a year in six months.

00:05:50.112 --> 00:05:51.570
We met with the
team that was there

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:53.350
at the time we got
involved and said, OK,

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:56.650
how can we shrink
this 15 million?

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:59.470
And they said, well, if we
do this, we can save 10%.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.450
And then if we do
this, we can get to 20.

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:04.470
And if we get really aggressive,
maybe we can get to 30% or 40%.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:07.500
And we said mm, and how
long is that going to take?

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.690
And they were like,
two, maybe three years,

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:10.940
if we're really disciplined.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.290
We're like, ah,
we're bleeding here.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.240
We can't wait that long.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.910
So instead, what
we said was, let's

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.300
move everything possible to GCP.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:23.190
And then everything
that can't move to GCP,

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:26.220
let's pick one data center,
and centralize there, and then

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.750
optimize like crazy after that.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:33.940
So how do you move 10
data centers into Google?

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.270
Well, step zero really is
pick your cloud provider.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:40.080
And it's worth mentioning
why we picked Google.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.830
Our customers, as I
mentioned, are telcos.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.270
They're very risk averse.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:47.650
And so they're dealing with
super sensitive personal data--

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.320
name, phone number, location.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:54.320
And so they can't
have data breaches.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.680
So when you walk in,
and you're able to point

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:00.010
to the huge investment that
Google has made in security,

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:01.690
that helps de-escalate
that, especially

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:04.210
if you can walk in with Google
people to talk about that.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:06.940
So that takes that
off the table.

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:11.140
The next reason we picked
Google was the native products.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.420
So we spent a ton on
Oracle every year,

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:17.050
and we'd prefer to
spend like zero.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:20.710
So we started
experimenting with Spanner,

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:23.140
and we found that we
got better performance.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:24.380
The price was better.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:26.080
And we got better
reliability, which

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.160
is a trifecta that you
just don't find very often.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.010
KEITH MILLAR: On that topic,
an interesting part of this

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:35.757
is this Oracle to
Spanner migration.

00:07:35.757 --> 00:07:37.590
And one of the things
you hear about Spanner

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.990
is because it's the
replication anywhere idea,

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.270
you've got GDPR
compliance issue.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.210
How did you deal with
Spanner in that setup?

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:49.480
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah, so
that comes up with most

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:50.950
of the conversations we have.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:55.490
And at least initially, we're
keeping people in one region.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:58.690
So if you want to
be in Europe, then

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:01.120
stay within the European zones.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:05.660
Originally, we were thinking
Amsterdam and England.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:09.070
But with Brexit, that gets
a little more complicated.

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:11.907
But it's just stay within
the region for now.

00:08:11.907 --> 00:08:13.990
And then as we get down
the road a little further,

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:17.080
we're looking at actually
leveraging-- what's

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.820
the new name for GKE On-Prem?

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:20.160
SPEAKER: Anthos.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.540
JAMIE SIDEY: Anthos, OK, yes.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:26.530
Potentially leveraging a small
footprint on the telco side

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:28.840
and then cleansing the
data before it goes up.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.790
So then there's
not even anything

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:33.309
that's supersensitive in GCP.

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:35.500
But there's ways around it.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.336
It shouldn't be a blocker.

00:08:37.336 --> 00:08:38.169
So that's step zero.

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:40.450
Step one is pick your partner.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:42.549
Then lift and shift
with a double bubble,

00:08:42.549 --> 00:08:44.710
which I'll get to in a
minute, and then consolidate

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:45.850
and optimize like crazy.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:50.360
So as far as the
migration partner,

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.130
actually, we looked at a lot
of vendors, not just three.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:53.797
But we looked at three
really seriously.

00:08:53.797 --> 00:08:57.500
And we picked Pythian because
they have a deep relationship

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:58.130
with Google.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:00.770
And that type of kind
of stamp of approval

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.300
was meaningful for us.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:06.270
And then they had the best
price and the best approach.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:09.343
And so, this is worth
unpacking a little bit.

00:09:09.343 --> 00:09:11.760
It's not that they were scraping
the bottom of the barrel.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.840
It's that they had a world
class team of really outstanding

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:15.990
resources.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.130
But it was an elastic team.

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:18.800
We didn't have to pay for the
whole team the whole time,

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:20.630
so that rolled on, rolled off.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:23.600
And then they also invested
deeply in automation

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:27.140
so that you make the
use of the team upfront

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.710
and then automate after that.

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:33.080
KEITH MILLAR: Yeah, I mean,
the 17,000 virtual machines

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:36.200
is a big number, so
we called it brave.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.700
It didn't really
scare us that much.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:39.242
If you were here
last year, you might

00:09:39.242 --> 00:09:43.220
have seen us talking about the
BlackBerry Messenger migration

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:45.480
that was done around the world.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:49.130
So very similar sort of
application footprint,

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:52.520
not as tight a
timeline as this one.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:55.680
The other good thing about
the sales cycle, if you will,

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:57.710
was how we were both
very outcomes focused,

00:09:57.710 --> 00:09:58.890
not talking about hours.

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:00.890
And it was very
outcomes focused.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:01.760
And we really--

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.190
Pythian saw this as a
great fit for both--

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:05.690
we have services
that we can deliver

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:06.680
on-prem and then remote.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:08.305
And we definitely
saw the scale of this

00:10:08.305 --> 00:10:10.870
requiring a remote component.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:12.790
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah,
and actually, riffing

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:14.790
on that a little bit, one
of the other things we

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:17.820
liked about Python was
they were very accountable.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.360
There were multiple--
actually, a lot of check points

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:22.095
for if we felt it
wasn't working,

00:10:22.095 --> 00:10:24.470
we could just stop working
with them and do it ourselves.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:27.230
And that type of confident,
accountable commitment

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:30.920
was enormously appealing to us.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:34.622
KEITH MILLAR: OK, so how
was the planning side?

00:10:34.622 --> 00:10:36.080
Probably three
years ago, you would

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:39.260
see a lot of people talking
about my cloud migration

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:40.067
methodology.

00:10:40.067 --> 00:10:41.150
And yes, that's important.

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:42.275
Yes, we have a methodology.

00:10:42.275 --> 00:10:45.530
It's just not as important
as it was back then.

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:48.320
Now it really comes
down to you really

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:50.000
need to leverage
native components

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.010
if you're going to have
a successful migration.

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.490
So what is the knowledge
on the native side?

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:57.380
The other one is
the execution side.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:59.640
You're talking 17,000 machines.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.350
So how are you going
to execute that?

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:03.320
So planning becomes
more than just meetings.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.560
It becomes a mix of what are
the human and software mixes

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.720
you're going to bring in
to actually execute it

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:09.880
in the timeline.

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:16.720
JAMIE SIDEY: Actually, we'll
get to this a little bit later.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.460
But we broke all of the
machines down into easy,

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:21.350
medium, hard, very hard.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:23.720
And the easy ones represented
the bulk of the machines.

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:26.920
But we'll dive into this
a little bit more later.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.280
So what is the double bubble?

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:31.370
This is the thing that
your CEO and CFO will hate.

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:33.770
So the idea is great, I'm
going to migrate to the cloud,

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:36.290
and I will immediately
start spending less money.

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:38.450
That is not what
is going to happen.

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:41.828
Let's say you're at n
or at 100 on this graph.

00:11:41.828 --> 00:11:43.370
You're going to go
from spending that

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.620
and then you're going to
ramp up your destination debt

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:46.910
at data center and resources.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:48.357
And you're going to spend more.

00:11:48.357 --> 00:11:50.690
And then you're going to ramp
up your migration partner,

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:51.982
and you're going to spend more.

00:11:51.982 --> 00:11:55.160
So you'll get from
n to 1.5n or 2n.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:59.420
And so on this graph, the
area that is above the 100,

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:00.720
that's the double bubble.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:03.890
And if you don't plan for it,
it will bite you in the butt.

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:06.740
And you're going
to have a bad time.

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:09.858
It might seem a little
counter intuitive to also add

00:12:09.858 --> 00:12:11.150
the migration partner on there.

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.580
You're like, ah,
that's more expensive.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:15.500
If you don't do that,
and we have done this

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:18.120
without migration partners
before with other companies,

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:20.775
it just takes longer, and
you end up spending more

00:12:20.775 --> 00:12:21.650
on the double bubble.

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:23.810
That migration
partner is definitely

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:25.760
a worthwhile investment.

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:27.350
KEITH MILLAR: Yeah.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:29.390
We experience this in
our personal lives.

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:31.895
Like if you move houses,
you have an overlap

00:12:31.895 --> 00:12:33.770
where you own two houses,
and you're probably

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:36.002
paying a moving company
in the middle also.

00:12:36.002 --> 00:12:38.210
I've never heard it referred
to as the double bubble,

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:41.720
but it is hopefully a
phrase that catches on.

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:43.550
One of the themes
we're going to get into

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:46.250
is just if you're going to take
on a migration of this size,

00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:49.380
it has to be not just sponsored
or supported by the C level,

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:51.230
it needs to be owned
by the C level.

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:53.460
Because this bubble is
meaningful amounts of money.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:55.148
I mean, this graph
is not to scale.

00:12:55.148 --> 00:12:55.940
JAMIE SIDEY: Right.

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:58.107
KEITH MILLAR: But it's a
meaningful amount of money.

00:12:58.107 --> 00:13:00.680
And if there is
delays in the process,

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:03.500
then you want C
level support for it,

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:06.230
that the destination is
worth the double bubble,

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:07.680
and stay the course.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.640
If it's just owned in
IT, for example, then you

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:12.800
can have C level
concern, anxiety,

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:15.177
a lot of awkward
discussions, that it's

00:13:15.177 --> 00:13:16.760
going to make it
more difficult to get

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:17.843
through the double bubble.

00:13:17.843 --> 00:13:18.890
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:21.350
For us, the double bubble
was about six months

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:26.570
and about $3 million, so
not a small amount of money.

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:28.130
One thought on how
you shorten this,

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.170
I already mentioned using
a migration partner.

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:33.957
I also recommend-- if you've
got multiple data centers

00:13:33.957 --> 00:13:36.290
you're migrating, you can
either say, OK, here they are,

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:38.030
and I'm going to take all
the machines of this type

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:39.530
and move them up,
and then this type

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:40.880
and move them up, et cetera.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:43.060
I strongly recommend
instead saying,

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:45.260
I'm going to take this data
center, the whole thing.

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:46.910
I'm going to move
everything up that I can,

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:48.500
and everything that
can't move, we'll

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:50.750
move it to our
consolidation data center.

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:51.950
And then turn it off.

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:54.462
You can immediately turn
off the electricity.

00:13:54.462 --> 00:13:55.670
You can not renew your lease.

00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:59.420
You can look at can I sub these,
shrink my footprint there.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:01.767
And you can start
to release the team

00:14:01.767 --> 00:14:04.100
or the portions of the team
that you don't need anymore.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:08.030
So you can start chunking
cost out, which, for us,

00:14:08.030 --> 00:14:13.210
with 10 data centers,
that was impactful.

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:16.550
KEITH MILLAR: OK, so we talked
about humans and software.

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:20.830
So we did use an automation
migration service for this.

00:14:20.830 --> 00:14:23.200
And a POC, so the humans
are important to figure out

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.570
the first ones, and software
is great at taking--

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:27.940
once it's figured out,
let software run it again,

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:29.050
and again, and again.

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:30.300
This is a bit of a busy slide.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.270
We broke it out into
a bunch of slides

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:34.420
so you can understand
what it is.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:37.720
But basically, humans
proved out each system type,

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:39.040
and that was categorized.

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:41.590
And then an inventory
was executed against.

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:45.207
So the inventory, it actually
wasn't that shocking a piece

00:14:45.207 --> 00:14:45.790
of technology.

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:48.160
I think it was just
Google Sheets that we

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:49.930
wrote these systems into.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.870
Some of them were super easy to
inventory and through software.

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:54.040
Others were very hard.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:55.840
And I think you're going to
get into that later, Jamie,

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:56.340
about that.

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:57.388
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah.

00:14:57.388 --> 00:14:59.680
KEITH MILLAR: And so that
was all written in inventory.

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:01.450
That was the execution plan.

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:05.470
Then the automation service
looked at that inventory,

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:08.140
pulled server names from it.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:10.900
A automation agent was
attempted to be installed

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:13.390
on the on-premise machine.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:15.730
If that was successful,
then you kept going.

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:18.190
If not, it was put in the
deal with later bucket--

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:21.190
basically, the human
attendant bucket.

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:26.260
The agent would then
replicate that OS instance.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:30.580
It would take it to GCS,
Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:32.980
Once that was verified
as a real image,

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:35.680
it was then taken
and instantiated

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:40.570
in GCE, Google Compute
Engine, as a VM.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:44.560
And that would be then flagged
into the inventory as OK.

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:48.520
And then not OKs were then sent
for human involvement later.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:50.260
And that's really how--

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:52.600
and it was an iterative
process because new inventory

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:57.790
would show up as the
process was executing.

00:15:57.790 --> 00:15:59.510
Engineers had to
handle the exception.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:01.302
So the obvious question
that everyone asked

00:16:01.302 --> 00:16:02.770
is what was your success rate?

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:05.770
And it's kind of
as you would think.

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:09.820
For the systems that were
quite well understood,

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:13.690
recent versions of Windows,
recent versions of Linux,

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:15.910
we had about a 90% success rate.

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:18.490
And then the ones that were
older versions of those

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:21.610
had about an 80% failure rate.

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:23.470
So you can imagine
where your humans were

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:26.470
spending their time, was
all on the exceptions.

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:30.490
And the sort of rote
Windows and rote Linux boxes

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:33.130
were very much handled
through the migration process.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:37.180
So that's how you get that
many machines moved in,

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:38.400
well, call it six months.

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:41.050
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah.

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:42.640
So some challenges
we encountered

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:44.110
during the migration.

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:46.570
If you boil it down,
it's the middle one.

00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:49.690
There is just too
much crap there.

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:54.370
We had a business that was
formed by multiple acquisitions

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:55.720
and bolt-ons.

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:59.950
And so we had more
than 50 flavors of OS,

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:03.100
all sorts of different
varieties of Linux and Windows,

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:08.710
but also AIX, HP-UX, Solaris.

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:11.680
So we said, all right,
everything that's on Windows,

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:13.990
upgrade to a recent version
that will be supported.

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:16.369
And keep only one
or two versions,

00:17:16.369 --> 00:17:17.619
and be disciplined about that.

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:19.450
Do the same thing with Linux.

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:22.210
And then for all of your other
flavors of operating system,

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.270
do you really, really need it?

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:26.200
Like really, really?

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:27.339
Can you shut that down?

00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:30.253
Can you move that load
onto something else?

00:17:30.253 --> 00:17:31.420
And in some cases, we could.

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:33.110
Can you refactor?

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:35.680
But in the cases where
you couldn't, fine.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:36.250
Keep that.

00:17:36.250 --> 00:17:37.270
Keep it on bare metal.

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:38.650
Consolidate in one data center.

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:41.740
So we went from 50 plus
flavors down to about 10.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:44.410
So we had 80% reduction there.

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:47.100
And then on the
too much crap side,

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:51.160
there is a mindset with
physical data centers

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:54.440
where, hey, I bought
all this stuff.

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:57.700
If I'm not using it, I'm
kind of wasting the resource

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:58.960
that I paid for.

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:01.820
So I might as well use it.

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:05.290
And if there isn't real
discipline on the team,

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:07.508
then you can fill that up.

00:18:07.508 --> 00:18:09.550
And then the team that's
managing the data center

00:18:09.550 --> 00:18:12.160
says, oh my god, we're
at 95% utilization.

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:15.130
We should maybe buy some more
hardware, so then you buy more.

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:17.290
And then you use
that, et cetera.

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:18.730
So the way we
handled this was we

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:21.850
did a physical inventory,
which takes actually longer

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:23.780
than we expected it to.

00:18:23.780 --> 00:18:27.330
And then we had departments
have to claim each machine

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:30.460
and explain why it
needed to be migrated.

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:33.500
And then everything that didn't
get claimed, we turned off.

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:35.290
And then inevitably,
hands were raised.

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:37.603
I do actually need
that, and here's why.

00:18:37.603 --> 00:18:38.270
And that's fine.

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:39.853
We're not trying to
be jerks about it.

00:18:39.853 --> 00:18:42.040
It was more just, hey,
let's not migrate anything

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:44.260
that isn't truly needed.

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:46.540
We cut our migration
effort by about 50%

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:50.020
just doing this, which
realistically, this exercise

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:51.760
probably should've
been an annual one

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:53.370
or more frequent
than that anyway.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:57.770
But it cut our effort a lot.

00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:02.370
Then tiny pipes, I'm sure
all of you have lots of data.

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:03.560
We certainly did.

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:05.600
And if you try and push
that over the pipe that

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:08.930
is good for day to day use,
it's going to take forever.

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:12.080
Strong recommendation is to use
the Google Transfer Appliance

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:13.177
if that works for you.

00:19:13.177 --> 00:19:14.635
So I think that's
in North America,

00:19:14.635 --> 00:19:18.050
and I think it's
in beta in Europe.

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:21.098
So we used it in North America,
and we used it in Europe.

00:19:21.098 --> 00:19:23.390
For those of you who aren't
familiar with what that is,

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:25.897
it's a big box that has
a bunch of storage on it.

00:19:25.897 --> 00:19:27.230
It shows up at your data center.

00:19:27.230 --> 00:19:29.960
You load your data in,
ship it back to Google,

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:32.690
and then they load it.

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:34.670
In India, where that
wasn't available,

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:38.020
we used a GCP partner called--

00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:40.270
I'm going to mispronounce
that, but I think it's Sify.

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:41.840
KEITH MILLAR: Sify, yeah.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:44.317
JAMIE SIDEY: And that
cut our migration time--

00:19:44.317 --> 00:19:46.400
increased the pipe size
and cut our migration time

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:49.045
from about seven months
down to six weeks.

00:19:49.045 --> 00:19:51.045
KEITH MILLAR: Yeah--
sorry, just if I can add on

00:19:51.045 --> 00:19:54.780
to the tiny pipes picture.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:56.580
From our teams, we
see this as being

00:19:56.580 --> 00:19:59.310
a chronic problem in
a lot of migrations,

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:03.900
is when you're moving that
many VMs, think about root

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:06.610
C drives, how much data that is
that you're going to be moving.

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:08.670
It's certainly not going
to be the bandwidth you

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:11.577
use on a daily basis, right?

00:20:11.577 --> 00:20:13.410
But it's also-- it's
not just the bandwidth.

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:15.600
It's the resiliency
of that network.

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:18.660
One of the things
we ran into in this

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:22.620
was a network equipment
component failure,

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:24.180
which it failed.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:25.347
We tried to troubleshoot it.

00:20:25.347 --> 00:20:27.805
At some point, you just have
to give up on troubleshooting,

00:20:27.805 --> 00:20:29.580
and we just put in a new one.

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:30.417
We replaced it.

00:20:30.417 --> 00:20:32.250
And I'm not even sure
if we ever figured out

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:33.720
what the problem of it was.

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:35.520
But we would have
found that if we

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:41.200
had run a two-day hammer test
to battle test the network.

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:42.837
And that's, I think,
sometimes there's

00:20:42.837 --> 00:20:44.670
easy to do the calculations
on the bandwidth

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.150
that you've set up
from a provider,

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:49.890
but do you have all the
failure points of a network

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:51.900
and making sure
that you have run

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:54.570
a multi-day test against that?

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:58.650
On the networking side
also, yes, the data transfer

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:01.510
appliance does work very well.

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:05.430
And we recommend using that.

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:07.490
It is kind of networking.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:08.760
Lock down the components.

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:11.970
Lock down security as much
as you can before you move.

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:15.780
And then, also, don't
give more internet access

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:17.850
than you had on-prem.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:20.100
Even though cloud
might tend to make

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:22.860
you do that, if you
have a limited amount

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:24.630
of internet access,
perhaps none,

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:27.810
don't change that
as you move over,

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:30.090
is sort of another
networking tip.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:33.300
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah,
totally agree.

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:35.260
Too slow, need robots.

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:38.430
So we had thousands of
machines we needed to migrate.

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:40.883
If we had done this by hand--

00:21:40.883 --> 00:21:42.300
as a friend of
mine says, lovingly

00:21:42.300 --> 00:21:45.030
handcrafted-- it
would have taken ages.

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:47.880
And there definitely would
have been human error.

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:50.310
And then the knock-on
effects would have been

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:52.360
unpredictable and not good.

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:55.080
So for a migration
of any scale, you've

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:57.210
got to invest in automation.

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:59.440
And then the dog
ate my password.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:02.460
This was a problem
with the inventorying

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:05.640
and with the automation.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:08.850
We were a turnaround
situation and bleeding money.

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:10.330
And we couldn't afford to wait.

00:22:10.330 --> 00:22:12.580
And so by the time we actually
started the data center

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:14.130
migration, a lot
of the team that

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:19.030
had been managing some of
those machines were gone.

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:21.900
So I think we had like
20% of our machines,

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:23.610
we didn't have the
passwords for so.

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:26.790
We had to send people into the
data centers to reset those.

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:29.010
And it wasn't like we
identified all of them at once

00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:30.060
and did it in batch.

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:32.850
It was kind of we discovered it,
and send someone in, and then

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:34.308
discovered it, and
sent someone in.

00:22:34.308 --> 00:22:36.150
So recommendation
would be make sure

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:38.070
that you have the
passwords upfront, which

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:42.740
seems like a no-brainer, but
it can bite you in the butt.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:44.350
So now I'm in GCP.

00:22:44.350 --> 00:22:45.640
Now what?

00:22:45.640 --> 00:22:47.980
So it can be a bit of a
runaway roller coaster

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:49.220
if you're not careful.

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.000
You've got a team that's used
to, hey, there's this resource.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:54.850
And there's almost
no incremental cost

00:22:54.850 --> 00:22:59.020
if I spin up more stuff than
that, to now, everything

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:00.280
you spin up costs you money.

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:02.990
And you can burn a
lot of cash very,

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:05.270
very quickly if
you're not careful.

00:23:05.270 --> 00:23:10.900
So first recommendation here
is, like you said, lock it down.

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:12.560
So lock down the
internet access.

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:14.470
But in terms of
provisioning new resources,

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:16.250
nobody gets rights
to do this anymore.

00:23:16.250 --> 00:23:19.120
It's centralized to
your SaaS ops team.

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:23.950
Have a request system with
a form or whatever, where

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:25.660
people say, I want this.

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:27.940
This is how much
resource I want.

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:29.533
This is the project it's for.

00:23:29.533 --> 00:23:31.450
This is the layer of the
application it's for.

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:32.200
This is my budget.

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:34.150
This is how long I need it for.

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.203
And yeah, it's a
pain in the butt.

00:23:36.203 --> 00:23:37.870
And there'll be some
pushback initially.

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:40.810
But then the team gets on board
with it, and they're fine.

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:44.650
If you don't do this, you're
going to have a bad time.

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:46.030
I promise you.

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:49.070
And this then ties into
labeling all of the resources.

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:50.590
And then you can
monitor and keep

00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:56.340
track of are you staying above
or below your budget target.

00:23:56.340 --> 00:24:01.620
As far as reduce and simplify,
we had four different support

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:02.850
ticket tracking systems.

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:04.980
We had eight different
source control systems.

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:09.240
We had 120 plus RDP boxes.

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:10.560
The list goes on, right?

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:14.460
So we said, OK, we're going
to adopt one ticket tracking

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:14.960
system.

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:16.620
And we're going to
move from eight down

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:18.720
to one source control system.

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:21.030
And we're going to go
from 120 RDP servers

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:23.430
down to four, et cetera,
et cetera, right?

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:24.840
Take that approach
everywhere you

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:28.800
can to converge on the one
system for a given function

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:33.330
and to make more efficient
use of the resources.

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:35.520
KEITH MILLAR: On
those, you had the easy

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:37.350
through most difficult systems.

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:42.870
And at the bottom, one of the
casualties was the HP-UX boxes.

00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:45.990
I'm assuming someone made a
compelling case that those

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:49.500
had to stay and had to move.

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:50.890
How did you deal with that?

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:52.200
JAMIE SIDEY: I don't
remember exactly what they

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:54.030
were being used for,
but I think it was one

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:55.860
of the source control systems.

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:57.810
And so we turned them off.

00:24:57.810 --> 00:25:00.580
So that took care of that.

00:25:00.580 --> 00:25:02.550
KEITH MILLAR: So just
back to the C level?

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:03.630
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah.

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:05.672
KEITH MILLAR: I need to
get to a destination that

00:25:05.672 --> 00:25:10.260
has a good cost footprint
and driving that through.

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:11.110
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah.

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:12.330
Yeah.

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:14.790
So once you've done
your consolidation,

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:16.275
set extreme goals.

00:25:16.275 --> 00:25:18.540
So I mentioned earlier
that the team that

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:20.580
had been there when
we got involved,

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:24.600
their approach was well, we can
get 10%, 20%, 30% improvement.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:28.300
If you set extreme goals, it
forces people to be creative.

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:30.780
They have to re-evaluate
their assumptions

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:32.640
and say, god, if I'm
going to get 90%,

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:34.187
how am I even going to do that?

00:25:34.187 --> 00:25:35.520
Maybe we don't need this system.

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:37.477
Maybe we can consolidate,
as opposed to yeah,

00:25:37.477 --> 00:25:39.060
we'll keep everything,
but can we just

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:40.830
be a little more efficient?

00:25:40.830 --> 00:25:43.510
So set the goal posts out there.

00:25:43.510 --> 00:25:46.950
And this is another place that
you need that C suite buy-in

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:49.380
because if you don't have that
and you set extreme goals,

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:52.972
someone's going to go
screaming up the flagpole,

00:25:52.972 --> 00:25:54.180
and then you'll get pushback.

00:25:57.080 --> 00:25:58.760
So how do you optimize?

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:00.150
First one is easy, right?

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:02.840
Take advantage of the
GCP pricing hooks.

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:12.260
So use sustained use discounts,
committed use discounts.

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:14.870
If you don't need something
to be outside of the US,

00:26:14.870 --> 00:26:17.230
keep your load in the US.

00:26:17.230 --> 00:26:18.950
You get the best
pricing that way.

00:26:18.950 --> 00:26:20.660
Those are the easy ones.

00:26:20.660 --> 00:26:21.960
Monitor everything.

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:24.230
So everything has to be tagged.

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:28.020
Monitor CPU, memory
disk utilization.

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:30.530
I used to work at
Dell 15 years ago,

00:26:30.530 --> 00:26:33.830
and Michael Dell's
big thing is--

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:34.340
what is it?

00:26:34.340 --> 00:26:36.090
If you can measure it,
you can improve it.

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:38.650
So make sure you are
measuring everything.

00:26:38.650 --> 00:26:42.530
And that way, you can track,
how much have I spent on this?

00:26:42.530 --> 00:26:43.550
What's my velocity?

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:46.860
Am I going to go above or below
my budget for the quarter?

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:47.720
And it's OK.

00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:50.580
If you're tracking, you go
above it, maybe you need to,

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:53.720
but you can catch it soon enough
that you can adjust course

00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:55.880
and dial back other stuff.

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:58.340
Or maybe you're going
over budget in a bad way,

00:26:58.340 --> 00:27:01.460
and you can catch that fast.

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:03.740
And then being
ruthlessly efficient,

00:27:03.740 --> 00:27:07.400
so re-platform Windows to
Linux, which is a hassle.

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:08.460
But do it.

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:09.260
You'll save money.

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:12.140
Delete unattached
disks every day.

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:16.350
Delete snapshots and images
as soon as they're not needed.

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:20.000
If you don't need solid
state, don't use it.

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:22.048
And there's valid reasons
to use solid state.

00:27:22.048 --> 00:27:23.340
So I'm not saying don't use it.

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:24.965
But if you don't need
it, don't use it.

00:27:24.965 --> 00:27:26.240
It costs more.

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:28.700
And then make use of
the recommendations

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:29.720
that GCP gives you.

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:32.510
They'll say, hey,
this machine is only

00:27:32.510 --> 00:27:34.610
running at 20% CPU utilization.

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:36.680
Maybe you should shrink it.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:37.520
Use those.

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:39.170
Don't rely exclusively on that.

00:27:39.170 --> 00:27:41.467
You're going to want to
do your own analysis.

00:27:41.467 --> 00:27:42.800
But definitely make use of that.

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:47.820
Automate.

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:50.460
So we've talked about
automation during migration.

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:52.300
Automation afterwards
is also important.

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:56.670
We now auto terminate
anything that isn't labeled.

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:59.370
Initially, we weren't
enforcing the labeling.

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:01.800
So when we put this policy
in, we gave people a heads up.

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:03.487
We're like, OK,
after this point,

00:28:03.487 --> 00:28:04.570
things will be terminated.

00:28:04.570 --> 00:28:07.195
And then we would give them, for
the first two or three cycles,

00:28:07.195 --> 00:28:09.150
we said, hey, these
resources aren't labeled.

00:28:09.150 --> 00:28:10.733
We're going to turn
them off tomorrow.

00:28:10.733 --> 00:28:12.090
Now we don't even wait.

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:15.450
If it's not labeled
properly, it gets killed.

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:19.230
Also, look for some kind of
standard patterns of use.

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:21.180
So you can probably
turn the machines off

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:23.597
at night that your engineers
are using and on the weekend.

00:28:23.597 --> 00:28:25.013
It's not that they
can't turn them

00:28:25.013 --> 00:28:26.580
back on if they
need to work late

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:29.130
at night or over the weekend,
but turn that stuff off

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:30.670
if it's not needed.

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:34.165
Look for other patterns like
if your testing team only

00:28:34.165 --> 00:28:36.540
needs the testing environment
in the first and third week

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:37.590
of the month, great.

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:40.590
Turn that environment
off the rest of the time.

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:42.990
And then use auto
scaling smartly.

00:28:42.990 --> 00:28:45.770
That can be helpful, too.

00:28:45.770 --> 00:28:47.540
On the second one,
go GCP native.

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:50.240
So there's two mindsets here.

00:28:50.240 --> 00:28:52.910
One mindset is I really want
to stay agnostic, right?

00:28:52.910 --> 00:28:56.630
I don't want to get fully
committed to one cloud provider

00:28:56.630 --> 00:29:00.222
because then I'm locked in,
and I lose pricing pressure.

00:29:00.222 --> 00:29:02.180
That's not the direction
we went because if you

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:03.763
do that, then you
can't take advantage

00:29:03.763 --> 00:29:06.140
of all the great things that
the cloud provider brings

00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:07.400
to the table.

00:29:07.400 --> 00:29:11.140
For us, with GCP, Spanner
is phenomenal, right?

00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:16.100
It's a game changer
for us, and we're not

00:29:16.100 --> 00:29:18.890
doing like a generic
it's a database thing.

00:29:18.890 --> 00:29:21.500
We're doing it's Spanner,
and we're using all the hooks

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:24.140
that we can in it.

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:28.160
We're also going full
bore on GKE On-Prem,

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:30.100
which is now called Anthos.

00:29:30.100 --> 00:29:32.080
The name hasn't
stuck in my head yet,

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:34.130
but we're going full
bore on that as well.

00:29:34.130 --> 00:29:36.980
So we recommend
going and embracing

00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:39.517
that the GCP is your provider
and really go full bore

00:29:39.517 --> 00:29:40.850
on what they bring to the table.

00:29:40.850 --> 00:29:42.650
They invest a ton of
money in this stuff.

00:29:42.650 --> 00:29:45.007
And it's worth using.

00:29:45.007 --> 00:29:47.090
KEITH MILLAR: Yeah, we get
asked this quite often,

00:29:47.090 --> 00:29:50.000
is how do I architect
my application

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:52.340
so that I can quickly move
from one cloud to the other?

00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:54.590
This was actually
the same discussion

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:58.260
we had back in the
database space.

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:00.500
How can I build an
application for this database,

00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:02.080
but move it to any other?

00:30:02.080 --> 00:30:04.550
Our advice then is
the same as it is now

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:08.150
on cloud, which is
if you architect

00:30:08.150 --> 00:30:12.830
your application properly and
leverage native capabilities

00:30:12.830 --> 00:30:14.900
of the cloud, you
will be better off

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:20.570
than trying to do some
heterogeneous shim

00:30:20.570 --> 00:30:23.452
layer that you can rapidly
move from cloud to cloud.

00:30:23.452 --> 00:30:24.410
You'll never get there.

00:30:24.410 --> 00:30:25.702
You couldn't do it on database.

00:30:25.702 --> 00:30:29.670
I don't think you'll
do it on cloud.

00:30:29.670 --> 00:30:31.650
So if you architect
your apps properly,

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:33.590
then you will spend
less if you decide

00:30:33.590 --> 00:30:37.730
to move than trying to bet
on the future of a move.

00:30:37.730 --> 00:30:41.050
Commit to the platform
that you're engineering on,

00:30:41.050 --> 00:30:43.890
and leverage the
native components.

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:45.140
JAMIE SIDEY: Yep.

00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:47.840
And this last point,
papers please--

00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:51.350
so make sure that you're
collecting the certificates

00:30:51.350 --> 00:30:53.480
as you decommission hardware.

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:58.610
You may get audited, and having
that paperwork is helpful.

00:30:58.610 --> 00:31:03.020
We did, and it was helpful.

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:03.740
So how did we do?

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:06.350
So we started at
15 million a year.

00:31:06.350 --> 00:31:09.860
We are currently at 4 million
a year after six months.

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:11.320
So we've saved 11
million already,

00:31:11.320 --> 00:31:13.750
which even factoring in the
3 million double bubble,

00:31:13.750 --> 00:31:16.360
it's more than paid for itself.

00:31:16.360 --> 00:31:19.520
And we're going to get to
1.5 million by next quarter.

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:22.270
So that 90% target, we
are going to hit it.

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:23.980
And that's all in, right?

00:31:23.980 --> 00:31:26.290
That's including our
GCP spend as well

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:28.940
and the team that's
dedicated to it.

00:31:28.940 --> 00:31:31.072
And if you look at all the
different other metrics

00:31:31.072 --> 00:31:32.530
that I mentioned
at the beginning--

00:31:32.530 --> 00:31:34.682
how many data centers,
square footage,

00:31:34.682 --> 00:31:36.140
electricity, et
cetera, et cetera--

00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:39.160
we've cut between 80%
and 100% on everyone.

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:42.425
But the big metric for
us is cash out the door.

00:31:42.425 --> 00:31:44.050
And this has been
enormously successful

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:45.503
from that standpoint.

00:31:45.503 --> 00:31:47.170
KEITH MILLAR: And
these 10 data centers,

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:50.950
that was sort of due
to the serial acquiring

00:31:50.950 --> 00:31:52.102
nature of the business.

00:31:52.102 --> 00:31:53.560
That's how you got
10 data centers.

00:31:53.560 --> 00:31:58.240
That's how you had so many
footprints, so many engineers.

00:31:58.240 --> 00:31:58.990
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah.

00:31:58.990 --> 00:31:59.950
Yeah.

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:01.530
I mean, we ended up with--

00:32:01.530 --> 00:32:03.920
I think we had three data
centers just in Germany,

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:07.690
which we really didn't need.

00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:10.150
And we had two in India.

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:11.500
We had one in Rio.

00:32:11.500 --> 00:32:14.860
And we had one in Kansas City.

00:32:14.860 --> 00:32:17.160
And it was just
all-- and Toronto.

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:18.790
It was all over the place.

00:32:18.790 --> 00:32:22.880
And the ones in India
were customer driven.

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:28.820
But the other ones was just--
it came with the acquisitions.

00:32:28.820 --> 00:32:30.350
So takeaways.

00:32:30.350 --> 00:32:32.670
Pick your partners carefully.

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:37.020
Clearly, we like
GCP and Pythian.

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:38.320
Plan for the double bubble.

00:32:38.320 --> 00:32:39.420
It's going to happen.

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:41.130
And if you aren't
planning for it,

00:32:41.130 --> 00:32:43.900
it will bite you in the butt,
and you'll have a bad time.

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:45.870
Do set extreme goals.

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:49.475
It forces the team
to get creative.

00:32:49.475 --> 00:32:51.600
And initially, there's a
little bit of a oh my god,

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:52.892
what the hell are you thinking?

00:32:52.892 --> 00:32:55.440
But it works out.

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:59.160
And then own it at the C level
if you want results like this.

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:05.720
KEITH MILLAR: Yeah, I'll just
add color to the governed lift

00:33:05.720 --> 00:33:06.220
and shift.

00:33:06.220 --> 00:33:10.030
Because I know
lift and shift can

00:33:10.030 --> 00:33:12.070
have a bad connotation with it.

00:33:12.070 --> 00:33:15.580
That can mean your CFO is
not happy at the end of it.

00:33:15.580 --> 00:33:18.590
This was what was called
a governed lift and shift.

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:22.600
So a lot of housecleaning
done beforehand.

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:25.635
Again, owned at the C
level and then [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:25.635 --> 00:33:27.010
You could argue--
I think, Jamie,

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:29.125
you did earlier-- saying
you could do this.

00:33:29.125 --> 00:33:30.500
You could have
done this on-prem.

00:33:30.500 --> 00:33:33.470
But what we see is
just people don't.

00:33:33.470 --> 00:33:36.220
There's no big rip in the fabric
that causes you to actually,

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:39.390
well, first, get the money
to do it, and secondly, paint

00:33:39.390 --> 00:33:41.780
a destination that's
really compelling.

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:46.030
So much like-- all
digital transformation

00:33:46.030 --> 00:33:47.590
now really means cloud.

00:33:47.590 --> 00:33:51.170
That's because you have the
mandate to go through this.

00:33:51.170 --> 00:33:51.820
So clean it up.

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:54.280
I mean, you didn't talk about
it on the previous slide,

00:33:54.280 --> 00:34:01.460
but going from 50 OS instance
types to support down to seven

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:01.960
is huge.

00:34:01.960 --> 00:34:02.230
Right?

00:34:02.230 --> 00:34:02.980
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah.

00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:06.280
KEITH MILLAR: And that
makes a massive difference.

00:34:06.280 --> 00:34:10.179
The other piece-- and I call
this the surprise invoice

00:34:10.179 --> 00:34:12.790
that drives CFO's crazy.

00:34:12.790 --> 00:34:14.920
You guys were very
frugal with who

00:34:14.920 --> 00:34:19.120
you gave rights to actually
go spend up more assets.

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:20.469
JAMIE SIDEY: Yeah.

00:34:20.469 --> 00:34:22.552
KEITH MILLAR: That's
critical, because even if you

00:34:22.552 --> 00:34:24.830
land in your destination
and the price

00:34:24.830 --> 00:34:28.690
is really good for February, if
it blows up in March and April,

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:31.010
then you haven't really
achieved anything.

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:34.120
So really figure out who needs
the rights to set up those

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:36.159
clusters, who needs those.

00:34:36.159 --> 00:34:38.949
And only give them
out to people who

00:34:38.949 --> 00:34:42.431
are accountable to the business
for that increased spend.

00:34:42.431 --> 00:34:43.139
JAMIE SIDEY: Yep.

00:34:43.139 --> 00:34:47.739
Yeah, and we did that right here
because we made that mistake

00:34:47.739 --> 00:34:49.489
on other deals.

00:34:49.489 --> 00:34:52.760
So that was a painfully
learned lesson.

00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:55.020
And then the last point on
here, monitor everything.

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:57.920
Do be ruthless.

00:34:57.920 --> 00:35:00.760
And I can't stress enough.

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:03.880
The default configuration
will just be to monitor CPU.

00:35:03.880 --> 00:35:06.490
But you really want to monitor
memory and disk utilization

00:35:06.490 --> 00:35:07.390
as well.

00:35:07.390 --> 00:35:10.210
And you want to have everything
labeled so that you can quickly

00:35:10.210 --> 00:35:14.080
dive in and say, OK, I'm
spending 2,000 more per day

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:15.250
than I was expecting.

00:35:15.250 --> 00:35:16.690
Where did that come from?

00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:17.560
OK.

00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:19.070
George, what's going on?

00:35:19.070 --> 00:35:21.100
Why are you suddenly
spending more?

00:35:21.100 --> 00:35:25.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

